A||||7||jlhuber||To put things into perspective ...||||4||
Z||000000||jlhuber||02-24-2001||12:11 AM||||(This was pulled from another automotive related UBB)<P>On 18 February 2001, while racing for fame and fortune, Dale Earnhardt died in the last lap of the Daytona 500. It was surely a tragedy for his family, friends and fans. He was 49 years old with grown children, one, which was in the race. I am new to the NASCAR culture so much of what I know has come from the newspaper and TV. He was a winner and earned everything he had. This included more than $41 million in winnings and ten times that from endorsements and souvenir sales. He had a beautiful home and a private jet. He drove the most sophisticated cars allowed and every part was inspected and replaced as soon as there was any evidence of wear. This is normally fully funded by the car and team sponsors. Today, there is no TV station that does not constantly remind us of his tragic end and the radio already has a song of tribute to this winning driver. Nothing should be taken away from this man, he was a professional and the best in his profession. He was in a very dangerous business but the rewards were great.<P>Two weeks ago seven U.S. Army soldiers died in a training accident when two UH-60 Blackhawk helicopters collided during night maneuvers in Hawaii. The soldiers were all in their twenties, pilots, crewchiefs and infantrymen. Most of them lived in sub-standard housing. If you add their actual duty hours (in the field, deployed) they probably earn something close to minimum wage. The aircraft they were in were between 15 and 20 years old. Many times parts were not available to keep them in good shape due to funding. They were involved in the extremely dangerous business of flying in the Kuhuku mountains at night. It only gets worse when the weather moves in as it did that night. Most times no one is there with a yellow or red flag to slow things down when it gets critical. Their children where mostly toddlers who will lose all memory of who "Daddy" was as they grow up. They died training to defend our freedom.<P>I take nothing away from Dale Earnhardt but ask you to perform this simple test. Ask any of your friends if they know who was the NASCAR driver killed on 18 February 2001. Then ask them if they can name one of the seven soldiers who died in Hawaii two weeks ago.<P>18 February 2001, Dale Earnhardt died driving for fame and glory at the Daytona 500. The nation mourns. Seven soldiers died training to protect our freedom. No one can remember their names and most don't even remember the incident.<P>------------------<BR>--Jess--<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/blackeye2/double0smile.gif">      <BR>Speed costs money, how fast you wanna go?<p>[This message has been edited by jlhuber (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000001||GT Bastard||02-24-2001||01:02 AM||||Point well taken... It's tragic that we can't remember the soldiers names.  We can thank the media for that.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||02-24-2001||01:34 AM||||WELL SAID. needless it is a shame for anyone to pass, but sometime it will come for all of us.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||02-24-2001||02:10 AM||||Damn, that's a shame. Kind of like the time I posted a thread about 2 soldiers that died during training in Ca and it didn't get one single reply but the Dale Earhart threads have like 50 posts. Not that it's not sad about Dale, he was and always will be a racing legend. I guess I might be the wrong one to say anything since I'm in the military. 2 soldiers have died here at FT Stewart since I've been stationed here, both incidents were totally preventable. One guy was killed while inflating a tire, the rim was what they call a split ring rim, the outer ring on the rim isn't held on by bolts so everyone is trained to take the tire off and place it in a safety cage to inflate. Well, this guy decided that since the tire was only low on air he didn't have to follow those warnings, well, he started to inflate the tire and the outer ring on the rim blew off and hit him in the head killing him instantly. <BR>The other one was out on field excercises, a supply truck was returning to it's company one night, late at night, the driver wasn't using his night vision equipment, he ended up driving right into a tank, the cannon of the tank pierced right through the windshield of the truck and through the driver, nearly going all the way through the back wall of the cab. <P>well, sorry, I got so long winded. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||02-24-2001||05:48 AM||||Oddly enough, I have been involved in some discusion of the Hawaii incident. The unit involved was transporting the 1/27th Wolfhounds. I help run a locator for Vietnam era veterans of the 1/27 and 2/27th.<P>A Blackhawk carrying troops overran another that was off loading a sling carried Hum Vee.<BR>Although night vision devices allow you to see at night they really screw up you depth perception, probably the most important single sense you need to fly a helicopter.<BR>Imagine driving a car with blacked out windows using a vidio camera and a tv monitor. Well it,s not exactly the same but similar. The military has a tendency to push technical ability past the point it works in my opinion.<P>Think how many, almost identical incidents, you have heard about on the news over the years including the Iranian hostage rescue attempt.<P>People view any public figure like Dale Earnhardt as if they know them personally. It,s really not an either-or thing.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Greg Piet||02-24-2001||05:57 AM||||I dont like to see anyone perish, but saying the government is under funded is a load of hooey!  Now money allocation may need to be modified and that is something different.  I have seen some Federal organizations pay ungodly amounts for items.  When a rubber foot available at any hardware store for $2.50 is sold to a US govt agency for $60 I just get more agitated.  Yeah If I were to pay $60 for a rubber foot I would want it to last a LONG time.  Or how about a glorified black and decker snakelight for $500?<P>I have seen ridiculous amounts of money wasted by many government agencies.  Heck one episode of "The Fleecing of America" will give one a fair idea about that and that is just the stuff that has not been deemed "top secret".<P>Not to imply that the death of the soldiers is a good or acceptable thing, but "Many times parts were not available to keep them in good shape due to funding." makes my blood boil.  Anyone else remember the famous $600 hammer?  Or the ash trays in the fighter jets which got replaced by a cover which was even more expensive?<P>Oh I had better shut up now before I burst a blood vessel.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">  <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!||||reg||1
Z||000006||2xsess||02-24-2001||11:40 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Greg Piet:<BR><B>I dont like to see anyone perish, but saying the government is under funded is a load of hooey!  Now money allocation may need to be modified and that is something different.  <BR>oh I had better shut up now before I burst a blood vessel.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I agree with you, another thing is that if the vehicle isn't safe to fly then it isnt' supposed to leave the ground. Our tanks don't move if they're not in good working order. But, the truth of the matter is that they're never 100%, there's always some part that hasn't come in yet or, the vehicle is just old and worn out, In my opinion funding should be allocated to buy new equipment and tools. Nothing is more frustrating than having to ask my fellow mechanics to borrow tools because mine or either broken or missing (not because I lost them either, they were never there)<P>Just my opinion.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||02-24-2001||02:53 PM||||I believe this is why we all stand while the National Anthem is played at pretty much any major event...<P>Something like this was said when JFK Jr. died in his plane crash.  Who cares, right, lots of people died that day too?<P>All I can say is if you don't like it or understand, turn off your TV...||||reg||1
